==English==
[[File:Rhithrogena germanica subimago on Equisetum hyemale.jpg|thumb|A mayfly, ''[[:w:Rhithrogena germanica|Rhithrogena germanica]]'']]

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|May|fly}}. Compare {{cog|stq|Moaifljooge|t=blowfly}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Any of the many [[fragile]] [[insect]]s of the [[order]] [[Ephemeroptera]] that develop in [[fresh]] [[water]] and live very [[briefly]] as winged [[adult]]s.
#: {{syn|en|shadfly|dayfly|ephemerid}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=John Gooderham; Edward Tsyrlin|title=The Waterbug Book|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=0_Paklse3XcC&pg=PA132&dq=%22mayfly%22%7C%22mayflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=cDesUuTwLMm8kQW_i4GIBw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mayfly%22%7C%22mayflies%22&f=false|page=132
|passage=Adult '''mayflies''' have large compound eyes and most have two pairs of wings.{{...}}'''Mayfly''' nymphs are distinguished by their three tails (two cerci and a terminal filament) and the set of gills on each side of their abdomen.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=Terry Hellekson|title=Fish Flies: The Encyclopedia of the Fly Tier's Art|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=zaP74hiL3hIC&pg=PA213&dq=%22mayfly%22%7C%22mayflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=cDesUuTwLMm8kQW_i4GIBw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mayfly%22%7C%22mayflies%22&f=false|page=213
|passage='''Mayflies''' are fragile, gossamer-winged insects that arise from bodies of water and often swarm in great numbers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Barbara L. Peckarsky; J. David Allan; Angus R. McIntosh; Brad W. Taylor|chapter=Chapter 9: Understanding the Role of Predation in Open Systems|editors=Ian Billick; Mary V. Price|title=The Ecology of Place|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=F-UZVzTh9O4C&pg=PA195&dq=%22mayfly%22%7C%22mayflies%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=cDesUuTwLMm8kQW_i4GIBw&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22mayfly%22%7C%22mayflies%22&f=false|page=195
|passage=Stoneflies have negligible consumptive (thin solid line) and behavioral effects (thin dashed line) on '''mayfly''' abundance. Consumption by trout decreases the abundance of '''mayflies''' (solid line), but trout suppress '''mayfly''' emigration (drift) and thereby increase their abundance (thick dashed line).}}

====Usage notes====
* In some English dialects, the alternative plural form is "mayfly". For exampleː ''While some animals take months to reproduce, '''mayfly''' need just one perfect day''.

